}
```

When the app resumes (user switches back), it calls `_hlEditor.setText(_document.getContent())`. This replaces the entire text content in the editor. When `setText()` is called, the cursor position (selection) is reset to position 0 (the beginning of the text).

2. **`loadDocumentIntoUi()`** (line ~118):


This method properly preserves cursor position by capturing the current selection before replacing the text, clamping it to the new text length, and then applying it back. The `onResume()` method is missing this same pattern—it's just resetting the text without restoring the cursor position afterward.

Looking at where `loadDocumentIntoUi()` gets called, I can see it's used when reloading content from disk in `checkReloadDisk()` and during undo/redo operations, but `onResume()` isn't using it. The solution is to apply the same cursor position preservation logic that `loadDocumentIntoUi()` uses when updating the text in `onResume()`. When `setText()` is called on the editor, it naturally resets the cursor to position 0, so the fix needs to preserve and restore the cursor position before and after that call.

The solution is to capture the current cursor position before updating the text, then clamp it to the new text length to avoid invalid positions. I should clamp relative to the old text length rather than the new one to prevent jumping when content changes significantly.

The key insight is that we only want to preserve the cursor in the normal resume case, not when `checkReloadDisk()` detects content changes and reloads from disk — in that scenario, `loadDocumentIntoUi()` already handles the restoration properly. The current implementation unnecessarily resets the cursor even when nothing has changed on disk, which is the bug. When content gets reloaded from disk, `loadDocument()` creates a completely new Document object and overwrites `_document`, but `loadDocumentIntoUi()` then tries to call methods on this null reference. The fix should preserve the cursor position when updating text in `onResume()` and only trigger a disk reload if the content actually differs from what's currently displayed. I see the real problem now—the current code always calls `_hlEditor.setText()` on resume, which resets the cursor to the beginning even when nothing has changed. The fix needs to preserve the cursor position by saving it before calling `setText()`, then restoring it afterward, just like `loadDocumentIntoUi()` does. This way, whether the content changed or not, the cursor stays where the user left it. The cursor position preservation logic is solid—save it before `setText()`, then restore it with bounds checking. This handles the bug where switching away and back resets the cursor to the start. Now I need to verify the `HighlightingEditor` class extends `EditText` to ensure these methods are available, then apply the fix to `onResume()`.

